# Fingerprints of findings gitleaks should ignore.
#
# Use the commit-independent three-part form:
#
#     <file>:<rule-id>:<start-line>
#
# A failing Gitleaks job appends ready-to-paste lines in this form to its job
# summary, under "What to do with these findings".
#
# Do NOT use the four-part <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<start-line> form that
# gitleaks labels "Fingerprint:" in the job log. PRs here are squash-merged, so
# the commit it names never reaches master. A four-part entry silences the
# finding on the pull request, then stops matching once the squash commit
# exists, so the push scan of master reports the same finding again and fails
# that build.
#
# A three-part entry still pins a start line, recorded as of the commit that
# introduced the finding. If a later commit on the branch inserts or deletes
# lines above it in the same file, the squash commit carries the finding on a
# different line and the entry stops matching, giving the same green pull
# request and red master. Nothing warns you, because the scan reads commit diffs
# and never re-reads the line. Use an entry here only for a line that is done
# changing; for a false positive that recurs, add a line-independent
# [[rules.allowlists]] regex to .gitleaks.toml instead. See CONTRIBUTING.md,
# "Secret scanning".
#
# Note that the scan reads each of the branch's commits separately, not the
# squashed diff. A secret-shaped line added in one commit and removed in a later
# one still fails the check, and editing the file does not clear it. The scan
# skips fork pull requests, which do not receive the license secret; reads only
# a pull request's first 30 commits; and skips merge commits, so a secret
# introduced by a conflict resolution is not read on the pull request. The push
# scan of master covers all three.
#
# This file previously carried four-part entries from PR #6800. They named
# commits that never reached master, both files they pointed at have since moved
# or gone, and the allowlist in .gitleaks.toml already excludes src/test/.
# Nothing was left to rewrite, so they are gone.
